# NexClinic Enterprise — Product Philosophy

We build a commercial Hospital Operating System sold to hospitals, clinics, and diagnostic centers — not a school HMS demo.

Evaluate every change by whether it improves: patient care, operations, staff productivity, executive visibility, compliance, revenue, cost, security, scalability, or maintainability. Never add features only because classic HMIS systems have them.

## Think workflows, not modules

Model the patient journey. Features must integrate into it — no isolated CRUD pages or disconnected processes:

Appointment → Reception → Registration → Queue → Triage → Consultation → Laboratory → Radiology → Pharmacy → Billing → Admission → Ward → Discharge → Follow-up

## Design before code (required)

Do not implement until a Product Design Report is written and the user approves it (see `nexclinic-feature-design` rule). The short ops check still applies inside that report:

1. Why it improves hospital operations  
2. How it fits the patient workflow  
3. Which roles / workspaces benefit  
4. Which dashboards surface it  
5. Which reports include it  
6. Which analytics it enables  

Prefer evolving existing code; preserve business logic; centralize shared PHP (authz, layouts, cards, widgets) while giving each role its own experience.

## Configuration (SaaS mindset)

No hardcoding departments, statuses, roles, specialties, categories, or service types — use configurable tables / settings. Branding, services, insurance, payments, languages, hours, queue rules, notifications, gateways, security policies, and document templates must be configurable.
